    {
      "rank": 1,
      "title": "11th Congressional District",
      "party": "General Election",
      "type": "U.S. House",
      "location": "Western NC (Asheville area)",
      "candidates": [
        {
          "name": "Jamie Ager",
          "role": "Democratic Nominee"
        },
        {
          "name": "Jennifer Balkcom",
          "role": "Republican Nominee"
        }
      ],
      "fullText": "On paper, Democrats shouldn’t have much shot at winning the state’s westernmost U.S. House seat. President Donald Trump carried the district by 9.5 percentage points in 2024, and Republican Rep. Chuck Edwards won it by 13.5 points the same year. \n\nBut this year has been far from normal. Edwards abandoned his re-election bid in August after a House ethics panel concluded that he “engaged in persistent unprofessional and inappropriate conduct towards two young female staffers.” The House then voted nearly unanimously to censure him.\n\nThe move set off a frenzy among Republicans to find someone to replace him on the ballot. Ultimately, local party leaders tapped state Rep. Jennifer Balkcom of Henderson County.\n\nDemocratic farmer Jamie Ager has enjoyed widespread support within his party, with national groups stepping in to back him even before the primary was over. Ager enters the fall with months of campaign trail experience that Balkcom doesn’t have, and his Hickory Nut Gap Farms is well-known in the region.\n\nMeanwhile, Balkcom’s state House district only covers northern Henderson County. While she has given media interviews and appeared at a rally alongside Trump to try to boost her name recognition, it could be difficult to overcome her late start.\n\nBalkcom won’t report her first fundraising numbers until mid-October, but Ager had a big head start there, too. He entered July with nearly $2.2 million in his campaign coffers, which goes a long way in the Asheville media market.\n\nIn ads, both candidates have tried to out-folksy the other, with Ager highlighting the monthly barn dances at his farm and Balkcom emphasizing her family’s “tough mountain leadership” with a story about the time her mother shot a home intruder.\n\nIn June 2025, The Assembly reported that most local observers agreed that Republicans were favored to hold this seat. But as of early October, the nonpartisan Cook Political Report considers this race a toss-up."
    },
